+# Large Carrier Multirole Civilization and Non-Specialization Contract
+
+## Owned Question
+
+**How can Symtropy let players inhabit, help build, command and transform enormous carrier spacecraft without reducing them to single-role classes, implausible personal possessions, or decorative city-ships?**
+
+## Core Thesis
+
+A large carrier is not a giant tool with one job.
+
+It is a moving settlement, industrial system, transport network, emergency refuge, political institution and home whose design may emphasize one mission while retaining a broad civic capability envelope.
+
+> **Large ships specialize by allocation, doctrine and history—not by becoming incapable of ordinary civilization.**
+
+A care-emphasis carrier still fabricates, explores, trades, defends, teaches and deploys vehicles. A foundry-emphasis carrier still houses families, treats injuries, grows food, preserves culture and performs rescue. A security-emphasis carrier still obeys civilian rights and cannot become a gun wrapped around disposable crew.
+
+# 1. Shared Large-Carrier Baseline
+
+Every inhabited large carrier must provide a credible minimum in the following domains:
+
+1. habitation and privacy;
+2. atmosphere, water, food and waste cycling;
+3. navigation, propulsion and traffic control;
+4. energy generation, storage and thermal rejection;
+5. maintenance, fabrication and structural repair;
+6. medical care, quarantine and casualty response;
+7. cargo, stores, logistics and deployment;
+8. communications, sensing and knowledge stewardship;
+9. governance, records, justice and dispute resolution;
+10. education, culture, recreation and spiritual or philosophical life;
+11. emergency shelter, fire control, evacuation and rescue;
+12. proportionate defense and safe-mode survival.
+
+A vessel may perform some functions through attached craft, convoy partners or host infrastructure, but those dependencies must remain explicit and failure-tested.
+
+# 2. Mission Emphasis, Not Ship Class
+
+Carrier identity is expressed through a **mission emphasis profile** rather than a rigid class.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- habitation and migration;
+- exploration and survey;
+- care and disaster relief;
+- fabrication and construction;
+- trade and cultural exchange;
+- route engineering;
+- hostile-world survival;
+- ocean and planetary interface;
+- security and convoy protection;
+- seed continuity and ecological carriage.
+
+An emphasis profile changes:
+
+- module allocation;
+- staffing depth;
+- carried-base mix;
+- reserve policy;
+- training;
+- mission reputation;
+- internal district plan;
+- political obligations;
+- maintenance burden;
+- preferred operating environment.
+
+It does not remove the common carrier baseline.
+
+# 3. The Seventy-Percent Rule
+
+No flagship carrier family should devote more than roughly seventy percent of configurable mission capacity to its primary emphasis in a normal civilian or mixed-use build.
+
+The remaining capacity must preserve:
+
+- broad survival;
+- repair;
+- care;
+- transport;
+- civic life;
+- flexible growth;
+- response to unexpected conditions.
+
+Military emergency configurations may temporarily exceed this allocation, but doing so creates visible losses in habitation, care, trade, redundancy, legitimacy and long-horizon resilience.
+
+The exact threshold is a balancing target rather than a magical physical constant. Its purpose is to prevent concept art labels from becoming hard gameplay classes.
+
+# 4. Commonality Without Sameness
+
+Some carrier systems should be standardized because shared parts and interfaces make civilization possible.
+
+Likely common systems include:
+
+- pressure-bulkhead standards;
+- docking collars;
+- cargo container geometries;
+- utility trunks;
+- emergency atmosphere couplings;
+- data and identity interfaces;
+- rescue beacons;
+- medical evacuation interfaces;
+- power conversion envelopes;
+- maintenance access conventions;
+- source-chain and Chronicle records.
+
+Standardization must not produce visual or cultural sameness.
+
+Variety should emerge through:
+
+- hull morphology;
+- propulsion architecture;
+- radiator form;
+- gravity strategy;
+- construction era;
+- local materials;
+- repair history;
+- internal city topology;
+- exterior work decks;
+- carried-vehicle ecology;
+- cultural decoration;
+- ownership and governance;
+- mission history;
+- alien or symbiotic integration.
+
+> **Shared interfaces let ships cooperate. Different histories make them worth remembering.**
+
+# 5. Scale and Player Relationship
+
+A player should encounter large carriers through several relationships long before full command:
+
+- passenger;
+- evacuee;
+- apprentice;
+- contractor;
+- vehicle owner using carrier services;
+- station operator;
+- department specialist;
+- elected delegate;
+- mission planner;
+- module steward;
+- co-owner or cooperative member;
+- captain or command coordinator;
+- founder of a construction campaign.
+
+The fantasy is not delayed until ownership. Meaningful access begins early.
+
+# 6. Ownership Is Not Occupant Ownership
+
+No property claim over a carrier grants ownership of:
+
+- residents;
+- workers;
+- dependent children;
+- machine persons;
+- attached living vehicles;
+- public archives;
+- medical patients;
+- refugees;
+- alien guests;
+- sovereign convoy partners.
+
+A carrier may be:
+
+- publicly owned;
+- cooperatively held;
+- household-owned;
+- chartered by multiple institutions;
+- financed through debt;
+- leased;
+- held by a route house;
+- operated as a commons;
+- privately owned with strong resident rights;
+- claimed by competing legal traditions.
+
+Operational authority, beneficial ownership, title, debt, public duty and mission command remain separate records.
+
+# 7. Command Is Bounded Coordination
+
+The command role authorizes decisions within a chartered envelope.
+
+It does not provide an omnipotent interface for:
+
+- overriding bodily autonomy;
+- ordering unlawful sacrifice;
+- reading private quarters;
+- confiscating resident property without process;
+- disabling escape rights;
+- forcing autonomous attached craft to deploy;
+- inventing emergencies;
+- suppressing safety reports;
+- converting public assets to personal loot.
+
+A large ship requires distributed authority because no one person can competently or safely control every system.
+
+# 8. Buildability
+
+Every carrier family may ultimately be player-authored and player-buildable, but construction difficulty scales with physical and institutional reality.
+
+The player may:
+
+- develop requirements;
+- select or design a hull lineage;
+- negotiate land, orbit or yard access;
+- secure finance and materials;
+- commission modules;
+- recruit institutions and workers;
+- choose standards and governance;
+- supervise trials;
+- participate physically in construction;
+- accept compromises;
+- inherit, refit or complete an unfinished hull.
+
+The player does not fabricate a civilization-scale carrier alone from handheld inventory.
+
+# 9. Failure and Graceful Degradation
+
+Large carriers should fail as networks rather than one health bar.
+
+Damage may cause:
+
+- district isolation;
+- reduced acceleration;
+- heat-budget restrictions;
+- water rationing;
+- lost docking capacity;
+- degraded fabrication;
+- evacuation of one neighborhood;
+- political conflict over reserves;
+- dependence on attached craft;
+- temporary loss of mission emphasis.
+
+A ship can survive while becoming less capable, less comfortable or less legitimate.
+
+# 10. Variety Requirements
+
+A production set of large carriers should satisfy all of the following:
+
+- at least four clearly different hull silhouettes;
+- at least three gravity strategies;
+- at least four propulsion families;
+- multiple internal city topologies;
+- multiple construction lineages and eras;
+- multiple cultural ownership models;
+- multiple carried-base ecologies;
+- different failure signatures;
+- different sensory identities;
+- no family whose entire identity is one profession.
+
+# 11. Anti-Patterns
+
+Reject designs where:
+
+- the hospital ship cannot perform meaningful repair;
+- the foundry ship treats habitation as crew storage;
+- the carrier is one enormous hangar with no society;
+- the military ship has no civilian or rescue obligations;
+- the seed ship has no ordinary life;
+- the trade ship has no production or emergency reserve;
+- the exploration ship cannot shelter people it discovers;
+- every hull shares the same rectangular module silhouette;
+- a color palette is the only source of variety;
+- one player presses every button while thousands of inhabitants become scenery.
+
+# 12. First Implementation Slice
+
+The first proof should use one carrier hull with three configurable mission profiles:
+
+1. Continuance convoy and habitation emphasis;
+2. survey and planetary deployment emphasis;
+3. relief, repair and refuge emphasis.
+
+All three configurations must retain the common baseline and visibly trade capacity rather than changing into disconnected classes.
+
+## Contract Maxim
+
+> **A large carrier is not a specialized ship that happens to contain people. It is a broad civilization that has chosen what to emphasize for this voyage.**
